er is started and initialized with a random number (drawn between 0 – CWmax) and the phy will be monitoring the medium and counting down the backoff timer (whenever an idle slot is sensed). Thus, the phy remains in the backoff state whilst the backoff timer is running. No further transmissions are possible over the medium whilst the UE is in backoff state. It should also be noted that the time at which the phy exits the backoff state depends on the availability of IDLE slots on the medium (i.e. this is a function of transmissions from other nodes sharing the medium). So, the backoff time could last anywhere between a few micro seconds (when the channel is lightly loaded) to a few 10s of milliseconds (when the channel is loaded, and other nodes transmit over the channel with larger COTs). Type 2 LBT scheme: This is a one shot CCA check and is typically used with grant based data transmission. LBT is required to be performed both for UE initiated (i.e. grant free) transmissions (e.g. RACH) as well as grant-based transmissions (e.g. data transmission). For grant-free cases (e.g. RACH) the type 1 LBT scheme is typically applied whereas for grant-based transmissions the LBT type can be indicated by the network. In the following sections the LBT aspects for grant based and grant free transmissions are discussed.

